# 14. Scaling Account Isolation

As the number of accounts grows, account isolation becomes increasingly valuable because it creates a predictable organizational model.

### Small deployment

```text
5 Accounts
 ↓
Basic account separation
 ↓
Manual monitoring may be practical
```

### Medium deployment

```text
20–50 Accounts
 ↓
Centralized account management
 ↓
Persistent state
 ↓
Task scheduling
 ↓
Account-level monitoring
```

### Larger deployment

```text
100+ Accounts
 ↓
Account registry
 ↓
Centralized state
 ↓
Worker management
 ↓
Task queues
 ↓
Detailed monitoring
 ↓
Failure recovery
 ↓
Resource management
```

These are architectural stages rather than fixed requirements.

The appropriate design depends on the workload and implementation.

---

# 15. Security and Privacy

Account isolation should also be considered from a security perspective.

Avoid placing sensitive information directly in source code or public repositories.

Sensitive information can include:

* passwords
* authentication tokens
* session cookies
* private API credentials
* private proxy credentials

Use appropriate secret-management mechanisms for production environments.

A public GitHub repository should never contain live account credentials or authentication material.

---

# 16. Account Isolation in AI Agent Systems

AI agents introduce an additional reason to maintain clear account boundaries.

Consider an AI agent that monitors multiple accounts:

```text
                 AI Agent
                    │
          ┌─────────┼─────────┐
          ▼         ▼         ▼
      Account A  Account B  Account C
```

The agent needs to know which account it is currently operating on.

A useful conceptual task structure is:

```text
Agent Task
├── Account ID
├── Objective
├── Context
├── Available Actions
├── Constraints
└── Result
```

This reduces ambiguity between accounts.

It also allows monitoring and decision-making to remain account-specific.

---

# 17. AI Should Not Remove Account Boundaries

An AI agent may be capable of making decisions across many accounts, but that does not mean the underlying account state should be combined.

A better model is:

```text
                 AI Control Layer
                        │
        ┌───────────────┼───────────────┐
        ▼               ▼               ▼
    Account A       Account B       Account C
        │               │               │
    State A          State B          State C
        │               │               │
    Worker A         Worker B         Worker C
```

The AI layer can coordinate workflows while the underlying automation system maintains clear account boundaries.

This separation becomes particularly important when an AI agent is allowed to perform actions automatically.

---

# 18. Practical Checklist

Before operating a multi-account automation system, verify that:

* [ ] Each account has a clearly identifiable record.
* [ ] Account sessions are associated with the correct account.
* [ ] Browser environments are clearly mapped to accounts.
* [ ] Network configuration is traceable.
* [ ] Tasks identify their account.
* [ ] Content history can be associated with accounts.
* [ ] Failed tasks can be traced to an account.
* [ ] Persistent state survives worker restarts.
* [ ] Sensitive credentials are not stored in source code.
* [ ] Monitoring provides account-level visibility.
* [ ] Recovery procedures do not unintentionally affect unrelated accounts.

